; Tutorial 1.02: Variables and Constants
;
; A variable is a named box that holds a value. Blitz has three basic kinds of values, and the last character of
; a variable's name (its "suffix") tells Blitz which kind a variable holds:
;
;   score    or score%   an integer, a whole number such as 7 or -120
;   speed#               a float, a number with a fractional part such as 2.5
;   name$                a string, a piece of text such as "Alex"
;
; You only need the suffix the first time you use a variable. After that Blitz remembers its kind.
;
; Works on: the original Blitz3D and BlitzPlus, and BlitzLLVM.

; Print opens a small window by itself, but this program prints more than fits into it. Graphics opens a
; bigger one first: 800 by 600 pixels, colour depth 0 (the best there is) and mode 2 (a window).
Graphics 800, 600, 0, 2

; Local makes a new variable and = gives it a value.
;
; The original Blitz also lets you leave out Local: assigning to a name it has not seen yet makes the variable on
; the spot. That is quick, but a typing mistake such as "lifes = 2" then quietly makes a second variable instead of
; changing "lives". BlitzLLVM warns about variables that were not declared, so these tutorials always use Local
; (or Global, see below).
Local lives = 3
Local speed# = 2.5
Local name$ = "Alex"

Print "Name:  " + name$
Print "Lives: " + lives
Print "Speed: " + speed

; A variable that was never given a value starts at 0, 0.0 or "" (empty text).
Local neverSet
Print "Never set: " + neverSet

; --- Integer and float maths ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Print
Print "7 / 2   = " + (7 / 2)     ; Both sides are integers, so the result is an integer and the fraction is dropped.
Print "7.0 / 2 = " + (7.0 / 2)   ; One side is a float, so the result is a float.
Print "7 Mod 2 = " + (7 Mod 2)   ; Mod gives the remainder of a division.
Print "2 ^ 10  = " + (2 ^ 10)    ; ^ raises to a power. The result is always a float.

; Int(), Float() and Str() convert between the kinds on purpose. Int rounds to the nearest whole number.
; Storing a float in an integer variable without Int() rounds the same way, but BlitzLLVM warns about it, because
; the fraction is lost without the code saying so.
Print "Int(9.8)      = " + Int(9.8)
Print "Float(3) / 2  = " + (Float(3) / 2)
Print "Str(12) + 3   = " + (Str(12) + 3) ; Text plus number joins them: "123".

; Integers can also be written in hexadecimal ($) or binary (%).
Print "$FF = " + $FF + ", %1010 = " + %1010

; --- Changing variables -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Print
lives = lives - 1
Print "After losing one, lives is " + lives
name$ = name$ + " the Brave"
Print "New name: " + name$

; --- Constants ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; A constant is a name for a value that never changes. Trying to assign to it later is an error, which protects
; you from changing it by accident. Constants are often written in capitals so they stand out.
Const MAX_LIVES = 5
Const GRAVITY# = 9.81
Const TITLE$ = "Variables"

Print
Print TITLE$ + ": you can have at most " + MAX_LIVES + " lives, and things fall at " + GRAVITY + " m/s^2."

; --- Global and Local ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Global makes a variable that every function can see. Local makes one that only the code around it can see.
; Tutorial 1.05 (functions) shows why that matters. Both can give the starting value right away.
Global highScore = 1000
Local thisRound = 250
Print "High score " + highScore + ", this round " + thisRound

Print
Print "Press any key to quit."
WaitKey
End
